WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:10.010
Protestors took to the streets of Hong Kong
beginning in April 2019, shortly after the

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.540
Hong Kong government proposed a bill permitting
extradition to China.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.310
Since then, Hong Kong Chief Executive Carrie
Lam (a position appointed by Beijing) has

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:19.820
pledged to withdraw the bill.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.890
But the antigovernment protests have evolved
past this issue

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.680
and protestors are now demanding more democratic
freedoms in Hong Kong.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.160
Honestly, it is hard to see how this will
all end.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.140
Even if Carrie Lam gets the boot, that's probably
not enough to satisfy protestors.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.800
And it seems equally unlikely that Beijing
will yield to their demands.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.760
The Chinese Communist Party does not want
to encourage similar behavior in other regions

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.180
problematic to the regime.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.600
The CCP also wants to show Taiwan that the
one country, two systems formula works.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.720
So far, China has avoided a Tiananmen-style crackdown,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.440
opting instead for repressive police tactics.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.220
Chinese president Xi Jinping has also
warned that anyone attempting

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.420
to split China would be crushed.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.680
How the Communist Party decides to ultimately
address the protests may change the course

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.840
of China’s relationship with Hong Kong forever.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.480
Which side do you think will ultimately get its way?

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.100
Let us know in our poll.

